sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/turing-capable-network-topologies--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Genericity for n ≥ 3 via counting non-sign-stable patterns and a 3-node embedding lemma" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/turing-capable-network-topologies> ;
    dct:description "Prove the genericity conjecture without first characterizing every n. Embedding lemma: if a principal subpattern on 3 nodes is Turing-capable (with a stable realization), and the remaining nodes have negative self-loops, then the full pattern is Turing-capable. Proof sketch: realize the 3-node block with a witness, give other nodes strong self-damping −M, and scale off-block couplings by ε. For small ε and large M, the eigenvalues of J and of J − k²D are perturbations of the block eigenvalues plus values near −M, so stability and the instability persist by continuity of eigenvalues (Gershgorin plus a Rouché-type argument). Counting lemma: among n-node patterns with all diagonal entries negative, the fraction with no 3-node principal subpattern from the n = 3 capable list tends to 0 as n → ∞. This holds because the n = 3 list is nonempty and the C(n,3) triples are only weakly dependent, so a second-moment (Janson) bound applies. Together these give: almost all n-node patterns with negative self-loops are Turing-capable. Weaker hypotheses on the diagonal (for example, at least one self-loop) need a refined embedding." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A theorem: the fraction of n-node sign patterns (in the stated class) that are Turing-capable tends to 1 as n → ∞, with an explicit rate, checked against the n = 4 enumeration." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The embedding lemma fails because coupling to the other nodes destroys stability or instability in ways small ε cannot control for some sign configurations, or the n = 4 enumeration shows a capable fraction inconsistent with the bound." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/proof" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. State and prove the embedding lemma (eigenvalue continuity under block-diagonal dominance) in Lean, or rigorously on paper if Mathlib's spectral continuity is insufficient. 2. Take the n = 3 capable list from approach 2 and prove the counting lemma via Janson's inequality. 3. Combine into the genericity theorem; state precisely the class of patterns (diagonal sign assumptions) it covers. 4. Check numerically against approach 2's n = 4 table: predicted capable fraction versus measured." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Eigenvalue-perturbation results in Mathlib are thin; the Lean version may need to be replaced by a rigorous paper proof." ;
    skos:note "Tool: proof" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: combinatorics" ;
    skos:note "Tool: enumeration" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-ddi> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-wavelength> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-morphogen-hypothesis> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2019.07.007> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1007/s002850000056> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/turing-capable-network-topologies> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Classify the network topologies that admit diffusion-driven instability" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2019.07.007> dct:title "A Comprehensive Network Atlas Reveals That Turing Patterns Are Common but Not Robust" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Scholes et al. 2019, numerical evidence that Turing topologies are common" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1007/s002850000056> dct:title "Turing instabilities in general systems" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Satnoianu, Menzinger and Maini 2000, Turing instability in general n-species systems" .
}

